    rationale: "In behavior analysis, 'positive' and 'negative' refer to mathematical operations (addition and subtraction), not moral valence. Positive punishment involves adding a stimulus (aversive) following a behavior, which decreases the future probability of that behavior. Negative punishment involves removing a stimulus (preferred) following a behavior, which decreases the future probability of that behavior. Both procedures decrease behavior — the difference is whether a stimulus is added or removed. Examples: positive punishment = verbal reprimand after hitting; negative punishment = removing screen time after hitting. Choice A is incorrect because it confuses positive punishment with positive reinforcement. Choice C is incorrect because the terms refer to stimulus operations (addition/subtraction), not social acceptability. Choice B is incorrect because the relative effectiveness of positive vs. negative punishment depends on the specific stimuli and context, not the type of operation.",
    difficulty: "easy",
